the Kenyan president's right-hand man  has his work cut out for him NCO quwata  will be responsible for implementing any  bilateral agreements reached between  Kenya's president Uhuru Kenyatta and  German Chancellor Angela Merkel during  their meeting in Berlin the agenda  includes development trade and  cooperation to boost Kenya's potential  of interest is Kenya's role as a  regional peacemaker and the kind of  support we get from countries like  Germany in strengthening that role and  strengthening our ability to be an  honest broker within within the region  the two leaders are also expected to  discuss tourism investments and trade  but despite the positive outcome  anticipated some Kenyans in Nairobi  doubt the bilateral talks will have  little real impact there's no security  structure in Kenya so how can the  president implement security agreements  with no structures here  he should have sat with Kenyans and  solved the problems here because this  kind of travel is expensive  he needs to solve the problems here the  Kenyan government is keen to see an  increase in German investments in Kenya  more German tourists visiting the  country and a boost in Kenyan exports to  Germany the last year alone we've done  close to 110 million euros worth of  exports into Germany and about 450  million euros worth of imports from  Germany Kenya and Germany have enjoyed  warm relations since Kenya gained  independence in 1963 whitea says he  hopes those ties will deepen in the  years ahead
 

